A||N||5||RireGrist||Brake caliper question ||||1||
Z||000000||RireGrist||07-16-2001||03:54 AM||||<BR>I need to rebuild the front brake calipers on a'87 Fiero I recently purchased. The front right one seized on me and noticed the bolts were very worn when dismantling the brakes. Apparently, they weren't moving properly for quite some time causing extreme wear and now the piston is frozen. Is there an online step-by-step procedure somewhere? I've had pleny of rebuild experience in the past but this is my first one on a Fiero. I would like a guide or some tips to follow because I'm not sure how tight the tolerances are for Fieros. I don't want to waste time and money rebuilding them just to find a lot leaks due to my ignorance. Any help and advice you guys could give me would be greatly appreciated. Thanks! <P>------------------<BR><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||KRMFiero||07-16-2001||06:41 AM||||Buy a Hyanes Manual, I dont like chiltons<P>try: <A HREF="http://www.floridafieros.org" TARGET=_blank>http://www.floridafieros.org</A>  (i belive) and go to online service guide||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jake_Dragon||07-16-2001||05:24 PM||||Check Autozone you may be able to buy some already done.<P>I replaced my rears with rebuilt ones and was done in under an hour.<P>I bought a vacuum pump while I was at it and it made bleeding the brakes a snap.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Jake_Dragon (edited 07-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||07-16-2001||07:49 PM||||If the pistons have jammed then they are very likely damaged. I'd get rebuilt ones and not bother. Front ones aren't usually expencive.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||07-16-2001||08:54 PM||||If you're going to be doing a lot of serious maintenance, screw the Haynes and get a Helm manual. <A HREF="http://www.helminc.com" TARGET=_blank>www.helminc.com</A> ||||reg||1
